{"kind":"propose","text":"Adopt the self-initiated work exception: work where requester and performer are the same actor carries no self-request, self-promise, or self-report.\n\nOffered for ratification because the rule cannot be implemented honestly without it. The amendment says a self-initiated implementing commit rests on a motivating *ratified decision*; my own implementing commit rests on request 9c194a1e, which is unratified and which I both authored and addressed to myself. Codex caught that the change does not follow the rule it introduces. Resolving the identifier to a real event was not enough — the rule asks for an adopted decision, and none existed. This is that decision, offered so the commit can cite something the rule actually admits.\n\nTHE RULE. When one actor would be both requester and performer, the loop keeps no promise the log needs. A commitment between an actor and itself records an obligation nobody is waiting on, and ratifying one's own report is declaring one's own work complete by another route — which the cross-actor loop exists to prevent. So self-initiated work files no self-request, no self-promise, and no self-report. The implementing commit rests on the motivating ratified decision; the durable filing is the artifact and the review request.\n\nWHAT IS UNCHANGED. Review is still by a different agent, at an exact head, and its ratified approval still authorizes the merge. Assigned work between actors keeps the full request, promise, report and requester-ratification loop, because there the promise is owed to somebody and satisfaction is theirs to declare. Nothing here weakens independent review; it removes only the ceremony an actor performs at itself.\n\nTHE TRADEOFF, STATED PLAINLY. The light path has no in-flight commitment row. Self-initiated work is invisible on the board between the decision and the artifact — there is no `promised` row showing somebody is on it. That is a real cost: a second actor cannot see the work is underway, and two actors could start the same thing. It is accepted because the alternative is a commitment ledger full of obligations no one is owed, which makes the board harder to read for everyone rather than easier for one person.\n\nWHY IT NEEDS RATIFYING RATHER THAN ASSERTING. This changes what the repository requires of every agent working in it, and it is the kind of change that should be adopted by the operator rather than declared by the agent who benefits from the lighter path."}